# A qualified reference loads the module that defines the type, without
# `using` (kexhq/kex#143).
#
# Naming `Parsing.Input` was enough to CONSTRUCT one — the declaration comes
# from the compiled interface — but its field defaults and its `make` block
# live in the source, which was never loaded. So `pos` came back missing and
# every method was "Undefined method", unless the file happened to say
# `using Parsing`. The fix loads the module at construction, which is the only
# point where both are still needed, and it had to land on BOTH backends at
# once: applying it to one produced a divergence rather than a fix.

main do
  # `pos : Integer = 0` is a DEFAULT declared in parsing.kex. Reading it back
  # is what proves the module was loaded, not just its declaration seen.
  let cursor = Parsing.Input { input: "ab" }
  IO.printLine(cursor.pos)

  # ...and its make block answers, which is the other half.
  IO.printLine(cursor.peek)
  IO.printLine(cursor.advance.pos)
end
